## Quick start

You need Python {{ python_version_major }}.{{ python_version_minor }} or later and [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/).

```bash
uv sync               # create .venv and install everything in uv.lock
uv run pytest         # run the tests
uv run ruff check .   # lint
uv run ruff format .  # format
uv run pyright        # type-check src/ in strict mode
```

`uv run` executes inside the project environment, so nothing needs activating. If you want an activated shell, run `source .venv/bin/activate` (`.venv\Scripts\activate` on Windows) after `uv sync`.

## Layout

```text
{{ project_slug }}/
├── pyproject.toml            # metadata, dependencies, and the Ruff, Pyright, and pytest configuration
├── uv.lock                   # the resolved dependency set; commit it
├── src/{{ package_name }}/   # the package{% if sample_module %}; module.py is a sample to delete{% endif %}
├── tests/{{ package_name }}/ # mirrors src/, one test module per source module{% if not sample_module %} (empty until you add one){% endif %}
├── notebooks/                # a starter notebook; ipykernel is in the dev group
├── .vscode/                  # workspace settings and recommended extensions
└── .gitattributes            # LF line endings on every platform, so scripts run in WSL and Linux
```

## Dependencies

```bash
uv add requests                 # runtime dependency
uv add --group dev hypothesis   # development-only dependency
```

Both commands update `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`. Commit both files together.

### Package index

{% if package_index_url -%}
This project resolves packages from `{{ package_index_url }}`, set by the `[[tool.uv.index]]` block in `pyproject.toml`, not from PyPI. Anyone running `uv sync` needs access to that index. Credentials come from the machine's uv configuration (a `uv.toml` with an index credential or the `keyring` provider), never from this repository.
{%- else -%}
Packages resolve from PyPI. To use a private index, add a `[[tool.uv.index]]` block to `pyproject.toml`; uv's documentation on indexes covers credentials.
{%- endif %}

## VS Code

Open the folder and accept the recommended extensions when prompted. The workspace settings format on save with Ruff, enable pytest discovery, and run Pylance in workspace diagnostic mode. The interpreter is not pinned; the Python extension finds `.venv` once `uv sync` has created it.

